# https://spdx.org/rdf/3/terms/Security/VexVulnAssessmentRelationship node742
node742 [ shape=none, color=black label=< <table color='#666666' cellborder='0' cellspacing='0' border='1'><tr><td colspan='2' bgcolor='grey'><B>VexVulnAssessmentRelationship</B></td></tr><tr><td href='https://spdx.org/rdf/3/terms/Security/VexVulnAssessmentRelationship' bgcolor='#eeeeee' colspan='2'><font point-size='10' color='#6666ff'>https://spdx.org/rdf/3/terms/Security/VexVulnAssessmentRelationship</font></td></tr><tr><td align='left'>rdfs:comment</td><td align='left'>&quot;Abstract ancestor class for all VEX relationships.&quot;@en</td></tr><tr><td align='left'>skos:definition</td><td align='left'>&quot;Abstract ancestor class for all VEX relationships.&quot;@en</td></tr><tr><td align='left'>skos:note</td><td align='left'>&quot;VexVulnAssessmentRelationship is an abstract subclass that defined the common
properties shared by all the SPDX-VEX status relationships.

*Constraints*

When linking elements using a VexVulnAssessmentRelationship, the following
requirements shall be observed:

- The to: end shall point to elements representing the VEX *products*.

To specify a different element where the vulnerability was detected, the VEX
relationship can optionally specify *subcomponents* using the assessedElement
property.

VEX inherits information from the document level down to its statements. When a
statement is missing information it can be completed by reading the equivalent
field from the containing document. For example, if a VEX relationship is
missing data in its createdBy property, tools shall consider the entity
listed in the CreationInfo section of the document as the VEX author.
In the same way, when a VEX relationship does not have a created property,
the document&#x27;s date shall be considered as authoritative.&quot;@en</td></tr></table> > ] 
# https://spdx.org/rdf/3/terms/Core/ExternalRefType/x509Cert node743
node743 [ shape=none, color=black label=< <table color='#666666' cellborder='0' cellspacing='0' border='1'><tr><td colspan='2' bgcolor='grey'><B>x509Cert</B></td></tr><tr><td href='https://spdx.org/rdf/3/terms/Core/ExternalRefType/x509Cert' bgcolor='#eeeeee' colspan='2'><font point-size='10' color='#6666ff'>https://spdx.org/rdf/3/terms/Core/ExternalRefType/x509Cert</font></td></tr><tr><td align='left'>rdfs:comment</td><td align='left'>&quot;A reference to an X.509 certificate as defined in RFC 5280 &lt;https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/rfc5280/&gt;. The primary media type shall be application/pkix-cert. The legacy media types application/x-x509-ca-cert and application/x-x509-user-cert may be used for backward compatibility, but shall not be used in new implementations. NOTE — RFC 5280 supersedes earlier X.509 profiles, including RFC 1422. Existing references based on RFC 1422 remain valid.&quot;@en</td></tr><tr><td align='left'>skos:definition</td><td align='left'>&quot;A reference to an X.509 certificate as defined in RFC 5280 &lt;https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/rfc5280/&gt;. The primary media type shall be application/pkix-cert. The legacy media types application/x-x509-ca-cert and application/x-x509-user-cert may be used for backward compatibility, but shall not be used in new implementations. NOTE — RFC 5280 supersedes earlier X.509 profiles, including RFC 1422. Existing references based on RFC 1422 remain valid.&quot;@en</td></tr></table> > ] 
